# Corrected Notes inline confirmation: private Chrome acceptance

Eight finite browser assertion groups passed using the exact corrected client `90886819feef847847fcae8c442e0a401a1146a7a4aa1fd862a54b5e01f41da9`, current Notes backend/support, current shared host and shell. The twelve copied source files and the separate current production client baseline remained exact. Corrected author 229 and independent model review 49 were strictly authenticated before and after. Their original bytes remain unchanged; only their four manifest/receipt documents are copied here for convenient review.

With a real attached note open, Tab wrapped between the inline dialog's buttons and Escape restored trigger focus and controls without any added API request or persistent change. Cancel likewise added zero requests while a genuine Save response was held after host commit. Releasing that Save published revision 3; a subsequent enabled draft saved as revision 4. A dirty draft refused organization before any request. Confirmed notebook Trash committed once at revision 5; repeated Enter/Escape/Tab during the held response sent no duplicate mutation. Restore Cancel preserved the trashed attached note and added zero requests. Confirmed Restore committed once at revision 6. Disposing the old view through the actual shell, reopening, and typing a fresh draft before releasing that old Restore response preserved the fresh draft and status. A later Save and reopen preserved revision 7 and the attachment link.

The campaign forwarded 39 browser requests (31 Notes API calls) to the actual host. Three initial synthetic setup calls and three separately recorded attachment setup calls yield 45 actual host requests. Browser Save requests were three; notebook Trash and Restore were one each. Three genuine successful responses were held only after persisted state was checked. Counts of requests, semantic groups and earlier model tests are separate.

An actual browser attachment upload was attempted through the documented file chooser. The Chrome extension refused the file because its file-URL access permission is disabled. No browser permission was changed; browser upload acceptance remains unproven. The synthetic attachment was then added through three explicitly disclosed private setup calls. The browser Download button issued one actual `attachment-get`; Chrome wrote a 63-byte file with the exact fixture digest `055503b5030e894c9ceaf9b0cd8feb8039569526fe720dc937442be4a82867f3`. The browser download-event bridge timed out even though the file existed. Its exact bytes, response bytes and creation time were authenticated, copied into this evidence folder, and the verified owned download was removed. Initial absence of that Downloads path was not observed; no unrelated file was modified.

The owned local relay accepts only its exact local Host/Origin and normalizes these to the actual private host. Method, path, body and session token are forwarded unchanged; hop headers are removed and content length recomputed. Request/response retention is bounded to 4 MiB, upstream requests to 30 seconds and the owned campaign to 20 minutes. This is a test policy fixture, with no production proxy-security or stalled-I/O preemption claim. No token bytes are retained. Expected favicon 404 and missing private Editor module 503 are preserved; navigating to the absent Editor module still exercised the actual shell's Notes disposer. No Editor workflow acceptance is inferred.

The preflight manifest-schema mistake (author `records` array versus reviewer `inventory` object) is preserved with exact source and raw output before correction. An attempted own recorder output-format adjustment via string evaluation was refused by the browser tool before any app action; the diagnostic and the two subsequently used recorder versions remain explicit. The first recorder's unsupported `inert` property observations are not acceptance evidence; actual AX exclusion and keyboard focus/control behavior establish the finite dialog behavior. Screenshots can lag paired AX/DOM observations: `final-saved.png` is retained honestly, while `final-reopened-proof.png` was inspected as the final visible body/attachment proof. The recorded revision status and actual persisted state support the Save result.

The owned Chrome tab closed successfully. The real host and relay closed normally with process exit 0 and the synthetic temporary workspace removed after exact path confinement checks. Production, author stages, reviewer stages and user port 4311 stayed unchanged. No ordinary tests, original applications/helpers, benchmarks, or full-suite runs were performed. This establishes only the stated finite workflows, not complete notebook/Joplin/browser/security parity or superiority.
